<cd>March 2024</cd>
<iss_url>https://www.aeaweb.org/issues/753</iss_url>
</issinfo>
<docty>Articles</docty>
<artinfo>
<ti>Optimal Inference for Spot Regressions</ti>
<augp>
<au><gnm>Tim</gnm><snm>Bollerslev</snm><aff>Duke U</aff></au>
<au><gnm>Jia</gnm><snm>Li</snm><aff>Singapore Management U</aff></au>
<au><gnm>Yuexuan</gnm><snm>Ren</snm><aff>Singapore Management U</aff></au>
</augp>
<pp>
<ppf>678</ppf>
<ppl>708</ppl>
</pp>
<ab>Betas from return regressions are commonly used to measure systematic financial market risks. "Good" beta measurements are essential for a range of empirical inquiries in finance and macroeconomics. We introduce a novel econometric framework for the nonparametric estimation of time-varying betas with high-frequency data. The "local Gaussian" property of the generic continuous-time benchmark model enables optimal "finite-sample" inference in a well-defined sense. It also affords more reliable inference in empirically realistic settings compared to conventional large-sample approaches. Two applications pertaining to the tracking performance of leveraged ETFs and an intraday event study illustrate the practical usefulness of the new procedures.</ab>
<art_url>https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.20221338</art_url>
<doi>10.1257/aer.20221338</doi>
<dataset>https://doi.org/10.3886/E192481V1</dataset>
</artinfo>
</head>


<head>
<pubinfo>
<pubnm>American Economic Association</pubnm>
<publoc>Nashville, TN</publoc>
</pubinfo>
<jrninfo>
<issn>0002-8282</issn>
