<cd>March 2024</cd>
<iss_url>https://www.aeaweb.org/issues/753</iss_url>
</issinfo>
<docty>Articles</docty>
<artinfo>
<ti>Hub-and-Spoke Cartels: Theory and Evidence from the Grocery Industry</ti>
<augp>
<au><gnm>Robert</gnm><snm>Clark</snm><aff>Queen's U, Kingston</aff></au>
<au><gnm>Ig</gnm><snm>Horstmann</snm><aff>U of Toronto</aff></au>
<au><gnm>Jean-François</gnm><snm>Houde</snm><aff>U of Wisconsin-Madison</aff></au>
</augp>
<pp>
<ppf>783</ppf>
<ppl>814</ppl>
</pp>
<ab>Numerous recently uncovered cartels operated along the supply chain, with firms at one end facilitating collusion at the other—hub-and-spoke arrangements. These cartels are hard to rationalize because they induce double marginalization and higher costs. We examine Canada's alleged bread cartel and provide the first comprehensive analysis of hub-and-spoke collusion. Using court documents and pricing data, we make three contributions: (i) we show that collusion was effective, increasing inflation by about 50 percent; (ii) we provide evidence that collusion existed at both ends of the supply chain; and (iii) we develop a model explaining why this form of collusion arose.</ab>
<art_url>https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.20211337</art_url>
<doi>10.1257/aer.20211337</doi>
<dataset>https://doi.org/10.3886/E193126V1</dataset>
</artinfo>
</head>


